><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>I don't see this plot referenced anywhere. And its a lot to take in without a whole description of the PCA analysis. Consider deleting</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="yes" page="30" rect="583.885000,54.128700,768.353000,171.852000"
/></highlight
><text color="#9643FC" creationdate="D:20260223165923-05'00'" flags="print,nozoom,norotate" date="D:20260223170005-05'00'" name="290a734d-970b-44c2-9ce4-20426e26e46d" icon="Comment" page="31" rect="385.841000,650.189000,409.841000,674.189000" subject="Sticky Note" title="Emily Liljestrand"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Very curious how the metrics in fig 30-31 are being calculated. If these are weighted averages based on species in the bottom trawl? And how growth rate is even calculated by a year of data in the bottom trawl</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="31" rect="583.885000,534.481000,768.353000,674.277000"
/></text
><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20260223172714-05'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20260223172904-05'00'" name="a050d0af-d995-4aa7-abb9-f3d88f64b16c" page="32" coords="151.027000,237.502000,555.303000,237.502000,151.027000,223.385000,555.303000,223.385000,56.693000,225.547000,385.285000,225.547000,56.693000,211.430000,385.285000,211.430000" rect="56.251800,210.989000,555.744000,237.943000" subject="Comment on Text" title="Emily Liljestrand"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>This is a little clunky and confusing. Especially the use of "supporting" which when you break it down, can sorta make sense - the species are supporting the diversity/range in the 1990s. But the way this is written out in clauses sorta jumbles the message. Consider just saying like, the same amount of diversity as in the 1990s or something like that</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="32" rect="583.885000,63.588600,768.353000,237.545000"
/></highlight
><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20260223172958-05'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20260223173001-05'00'" name="99622ea6-d6d3-4624-90cd-884916e13b12" page="32" coords="286.674000,183.704000,289.442000,183.704000,286.674000,169.587000,289.442000,169.587000" rect="286.233000,169.146000,289.883000,184.145000" subject="Comment on Text" title="Emily Liljestrand"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>delete comma</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="yes" page="32" rect="612.000000,91.703900,796.000000,183.704000"
/></highlight
><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20260219085407-05'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20260219085439-05'00'" name="2516906b-3548-42ba-95d6-c1835efa6d42" page="33" coords="281.818000,412.974000,324.186000,412.974000,281.818000,398.857000,324.186000,398.857000" rect="281.377000,398.416000,324.627000,413.415000" subject="Comment on Text" title="Kiersten L Curti"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:24.4.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.5pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>According to Figure 34, it seems like the northward trend is not significant (no orange line) and therefore should not be mentioned here.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="33" rect="499.199000,314.183000,682.908000,412.801000"
/></highlight
><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20260219094937-05'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20260219095044-05'00'" name="be2bf009-26a7-4338-8de7-3179b39237de" page="33" coords="496.566000,606.242000,555.312000,606.242000,496.566000,592.125000,555.312000,592.125000,56.693000,594.287000,555.312000,594.287000,56.693000,580.170000,555.312000,580.170000,56.693000,582.332000,192.798000,582.332000,56.693000,568.215000,192.798000,568.215000" rect="56.251800,567.774000,555.753000,606.683000" subject="Comment on Text" title="Kiersten L Curti"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:24.4.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.5pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>For species that extend past the BTS footprint and well into Canada (ex: mackerel are common in Newfoundland), is there a way we can get at this potential shift in availability?</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="33" rect="499.199000,496.146000,682.908000,606.109000"
/></highlight
><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20260219090730-05'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20260219090752-05'00'" name="18af1ffa-462c-48f7-86f4-3938bf1238cb" page="35" coords="244.254000,290.558000,488.863000,290.558000,244.254000,276.441000,488.863000,276.441000" rect="243.813000,276.000000,489.304000,290.999000" subject="Comment on Text" title="Kiersten L Curti"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:24.4.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.5pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Why isn't there a figure for small copepods, when the trends are signficant?</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="35" rect="612.000000,198.558000,796.000000,290.558000"
/></highlight
><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20260227155832-05'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20260227160012-05'00'" name="23fdb149-0d9b-4283-86be-24c8490388de" page="35" coords="134.508000,302.513000,347.041000,302.513000,134.508000,288.396000,347.041000,288.396000" rect="134.067000,287.955000,347.482000,302.954000" subject="Comment on Text" title="Harvey Walsh"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Was this index calculated using multiple taxon? Previously, it was only Calanus finmarchicus abundance.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="35" rect="612.000000,210.513000,796.000000,302.513000"
/></highlight
><strikeout color="#F86464" creationdate="D:20260302073059-05'00'" flags="print" date="D:20260302073059-05'00'" name="2923a8c5-ef6b-4fe2-be41-f278303761f2" page="36" coords="221.387000,160.929000,532.008000,160.929000,221.387000,148.197000,532.008000,148.197000" rect="217.590000,147.401000,535.805000,161.725000" subject="Cross-Out" title="Kristan Blackhart"
